The genealogy of Jesus' family.
This is the genealogy of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham.
Abraham was a father of Isaac. And Isaac was a father of Jacob. And Jacob was a father of Judah and his brothers.
Judah was the father of Periz and Zerah. Their mother was Tamar. Periz was the father of Hezron. Hezron was the father of Ram.
He was the father of Amminadab. Amminadab was the father of Nachshon. Nachshon was the father of Salmon.
Salmon was the father of Boaz. Rahab was the mother of Boaz. Boaz was the father of Obed. Ruth was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse.
And Jesse was the father of king David. And David was the father of Solomon. And the mother of Solomon was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon was the father of Rehoboam. Rehoboam was the father of Abijah. Abijah was the father of Asa.
Jasah was the father of Jehoshaphat. And Jehoshaphat was the father of Joram. And Joram was the father of Uzziah.
Uzziah the father of Jotham, Jotham the father of Ahaz, Ahaz the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh, and Manasseh was the father of Amon, and Amon was the father of Josiah.
Josiah was the father of Jehoiakim and his brothers. At that time, the Jewish people were forced to leave for Babylon.
After that, the family line continued. Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel was the father of Abiud. Abiud was the father of Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of Azor.
Aser was the father of Sdoq. Sdoq was the father of AChim. AChim was the father of Ayyowd.
Eliphaz was the father of Eliphelet. Eliphelet was the father of Shlomo. Shlomo was the father of Rechavam. Rechavam was the father of Aviyah. Aviyah was the father of Asa. Asa was the father of Yehoshafat. Yehoshafat was the father of Yehonatan. Yehonatan was the father of Yoash. Yoash was the father of Amaziah. Amaziah was the father of Azaryah. Azaryah was the father of Yehioyakim. Yehioyakim was the father of Yehioyashin.
Jacob was the father of Joseph. Joseph was the husband of Mary. Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
Therefore, there were fourteen generations from Abraham to David. And fourteen generations from David until the Jewish people were forced to leave for Babylon. And fourteen generations from that time to Christ. Jesus Christ was born.
Thus the birth of Jesus Christ came about. His mother Mary was pledged to be married to Joseph. Before they came together, it was found that she was with child. Mary was pledged to be married to Joseph, and she was found to be pregnant before they came together. Since Joseph her husband was a righteous man and did not want to expose her to public disgrace, he had in mind to divorce her quietly.
Her husband Joseph was a righteous man. He did not want to expose her publicly. So he planned to quietly divorce her.
But while Joseph was pondering these things, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. "Joseph, son of David," the angel said, "do not be afraid to take Mary as your wife, for the child conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit."
She will give birth to a son. You must name him Jesus. This is because he will save his people from their sins.
All this happened to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet,
The virgin will conceive and give birth to a son, and she will call him Immanuel. (Isaiah 7:14) And Immanuel means "God with us."
Joseph awoke. He did what the angel of the Lord commanded him. He took Mary into his house as his wife.
But he did not have intimate relations with her until she gave birth to a son. And he named him Jesus.
The Visit of the Wise Men to Jesus
Jesus was born in Bethlehem of Judea. This took place during the time when Herod was king over Judea. And after Jesus was born, wise men from the East came to Jerusalem.
They asked, "Where is the child who has been born to be the king of the Jews? For we have seen his star in the east and have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard this, he was deeply disturbed, and all the people of Jerusalem were also disturbed.
Herod called together all the chief priests of the people. He also sent for the teachers of the law. He asked them, "Where is the Messiah to be born?"
"In Bethlehem of Judea," they answered him. "For thus it is written through the prophet: 'But you, Bethlehem, in the land of Judah, are not the least among the rulers of Judah; for out of you will come a ruler who will shepherd my people Israel.'"
"أما أنت يا بيت لحم أفراتة، صغيرة أن تكوني بين رؤساء يهوذا، فمنك يخرج لي الذي يكون متسلطا على إسرائيل، ومخارجه منذ القديم، منذ أيام الأزل." (ميخا 5: 2)
Then Herod secretly called the wise men and ascertained from them the exact time when the star appeared.
Send them to Bethlehem. And he said, "Go! Search carefully for the child. And when you find him, let me know. Then I can go and worship him too."
After the wise men had listened to the king, they set out on their journey. And the star that they had seen in the east went before them. And finally, it stopped over the place where the child was.
When they saw the star, they were filled with joy.
The wise men went to the house. There they saw the child with his mother Mary. They prostrated to him and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. They presented to him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their country by another road. The flight of the Holy Family into Egypt.
After the wise men left, Joseph had a dream vision. An angel of the Lord appeared to him in the dream. And the angel said to him, "Rise, take the child and his mother and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you to return. Herod is going to search for the child. He wants to kill him."
Joseph got up. And at night, he left for Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
They remained there until King Herod died. Thus the words of the Lord, which he spoke through the prophet, were fulfilled. For he had said, "I have surely called my son out of Egypt." (Hosea 11:1)
Herod realized that the wise men had deceived him. He became furiously angry. He issued orders concerning Bethlehem and the surrounding area. He commanded that all the boy children who were two years old or less were to be killed. This timing coincided with the time that the wise men had seen the star.
Thus were the words of the prophet Jeremiah fulfilled. He had said,
A voice is heard in Ramah. It is the voice of weeping and great sorrow. Rachel weeps for her children. She refuses to be comforted, because they are gone. (Jeremiah 31:15) The family of Jesus returns to Nazareth.
After Herod's death, Joseph saw a dream while he was still in Egypt. An angel of the Lord appeared to him in the dream.
The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother. Go to the land of Israel. Those who tried to kill the child are dead."
Then Joseph arose and took the Child and His mother Mary and returned to the land of Israel.
But afterward he heard that Archelaus had become king over Judea, and he was ruling in the place of his father Herod. So Joseph became afraid to go there. Then an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream, and he returned to the land of Galilee.
He lived there in a town called Nazareth. And so the words of the prophets about Jesus were fulfilled. They said, "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist paves the way.
In those days, John the Baptist came and preached in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Repent of your sins! For the Kingdom of Heaven is at hand."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke. He said, "A voice of one calling in the wilderness, 'Prepare the way for the Lord; make straight paths for him.'' (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of camel hair. He wore a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
People came out to him from Jerusalem and all Judea, and all the region around the Jordan.
When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the Jordan River.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to where he was baptizing. He said to them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the wrath to come?"
Bring forth fruit meet for repentance.
Do not think and say to yourselves, 'Our father is Abraham.' I say to you, God is able from these stones to raise up children to Abraham.
The axe is already lying at the roots of the trees. All the trees that do not bear good fruit will be cut down. They will be thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water, calling you to repent of your sins. But after me comes one who is mightier than I, and I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and with fire.
He carries his sickle in his hand to remove the straw from his threshing floor. He will gather his wheat in the granary. But he will burn the chaff with unquenchable fire.
Jesus came from Galilee to the River Jordan. He wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to prevent him. And he said to Jesus, "I need to be baptized by you. Why do you come to me?"
Jesus answered, "Be it so now. For it is fitting to do this. He is fulfilling God's holy plan." Then John agreed.
As soon as Jesus was baptized, he came up out of the water. At that moment, the heavens were opened. And Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove upon him.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my son, whom I love; I am well pleased with him."
Jesus is tempted.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness. There, the devil tempted him.
After forty days and forty nights of fasting, Jesus was hungry.
The tempter came and said, "If you are the Son of God, tell this stone to become bread."
Jesus answered, saying, "It is written: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city. He led him to the pinnacle of the temple, at the edge of it.
He said, "If you are the Son of God, throw yourself down, for it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you, and they will lift you up in their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' " (Psalm 91:11, 12)
Jesus answered and said to him, "It is written again, 'You shall not tempt the Lord your God.' " (Deuteronomy 6:16)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
He said, "If you submit and bow down to me, I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan! For it is written: 'Worship the Lord your God, and serve him only.' " (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil left Jesus. And angels came and took care of him. Jesus began to preach.
John was put in prison; and when Jesus heard of it, he departed again into Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth. He went to live in the city of Capernaum. It was located on the banks of the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
This is how the prophecy of the prophet Isaiah was fulfilled. He said:
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee, where non-Jews live! Land along the Mediterranean Sea! Territory east of the Jordan River!
The people walking in darkness have seen a great light; on those living in a land of deep darkness, a light has dawned.
Since then, Jesus began preaching. "Repent of your sins!" he said. "The Kingdom of Heaven is at hand." Jesus chose his first disciples.
One day, Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come. Follow me." "I will make you fishers of men."
So they left their nets immediately and followed him.
After that, he saw two other brothers. They were James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. And as they were preparing to cast their nets, Jesus called them.
Immediately they left the boat and their father and followed Jesus. Jesus healed the sick.
Jesus traveled throughout Galilee. There, he taught in the synagogues. He preached the good news of the Kingdom of God. And he healed all the diseases and afflictions that the people had.
News about him spread throughout Syria. People brought him everyone who was sick with various diseases. Some were suffering from severe pain. While others were controlled by demons. Some were trembling violently. And others could not move at all. And Jesus healed everyone.
Large crowds followed him. Some came from Galilee, from the region known as the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem and Judea. While others came from the region located across the Jordan River.
Jesus bestows blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds. He went up into a mountain, and when he had sat down, his disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. And he said,
The meek are those who feel the spiritual need. To them belongs the Kingdom of Heaven.
Blessed are the meek, for they will inherit the earth.
Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are the hungry and thirsty for righteousness, for they will be filled.
Blessed are the merciful, for they shall be shown mercy.
Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God.
Blessed are those who mourn for justice, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are you when men revile you and persecute you falsely on my account. Rejoice and be exceeding glad, for great is your reward in heaven, for so persecuted they the prophets who were before you.
Rejoice and be glad. Your reward in heaven is great. Thus were the prophets who were before you persecuted. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But what if the salt loses its saltiness? How can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything. It will be thrown out. People will trample it under their feet.
You are the light of the world. No one lights a lamp and puts it under a basket, but rather on a stand, so that those who come in can see the light.
No one lights a lamp and puts it under a basket, but on a lampstand, so that those who come in may see the light.
"Let your light so shine before men, that they may see your good works and glorify your Father who is in heaven. Jesus fulfills the law and gives it its true meaning."
Do not think that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets. I have not come to abolish but to fulfill.
Here is what I am about to tell you is truly real. Heaven and earth will disappear before even the smallest letter of the law disappears. Not even the least stroke of a pen of the law will disappear until all is accomplished.
Do not neglect even one of the least commandments. And do not teach others to neglect them. For if you do, you will be called the least important in the Kingdom of Heaven. Rather, practice and teach these commandments. And you will be called important in the Kingdom of Heaven.
This is what I tell you. You must be more righteous than the Pharisees and teachers of the law. Otherwise, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven. To murder
You have heard what was said to people in ancient times. It was said to them, "You shall not murder. (Exodus 20:13) Everyone who commits murder will be accountable for it."
But let me tell you this. Do not be angry with your brother. Everyone who is angry with his brother is subject to judgment. Again, everyone who says to his brother, "Raca," must be held accountable before the Sanhedrin. But whoever says, "You fool!" will be in danger of the fire of hell.
Assume you are presenting your gift at the altar. And you remember that your brother has something against you.
Put your gift before the altar. First, go and be reconciled to your brother, and then come and offer your gift.
Assume someone has a claim against you and takes you to court. Settle the matter quickly. Do so while you are still with him on your way. Otherwise, he may hand you over to the judge. And the judge may hand you over to the officer. And you may end up in prison.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. You will not leave until you pay the last penny! Adultery
You have heard that it was said, "You shall not commit adultery." (Exodus 20:14)
But let me tell you this. Do not look at a woman with lust. Whoever does this has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to stumble into sin, pluck it out and throw it away. For your eye is only a small part of your body. It is better for you to lose it than to have your whole body thrown into hell.
If your right hand tempts you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. Your hand is merely a small part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your entire body thrown into the fire. Divorce.
It was said, "When a man puts away his wife, giving her a certificate of divorce" (Deuteronomy 24:1)
But let me tell you this. Whoever divorces his wife causes her to commit adultery. And whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. A man should not divorce his wife unless she is unfaithful to him. Oaths.
Again, you have heard what was said to your people long ago. They were told, "Do not break your covenant with the Lord your God, and do not make for yourselves molten gods."
But let me tell you something. Never make any promises like that at all. Don't make them in heaven's name. That is God's throne.
Do not make them in the name of the earth; for it is the footstool of God. Nor in the name of Jerusalem; for it is the city of the great King.
And do not swear by your heads, for you cannot make even one hair white or black.
Let "yes" mean "yes" only. Let "no" mean "no". Anything more than that comes from the devil. Be kind to others.
You have heard that it was said, "Eye for eye, and tooth for tooth." (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21)
But let me tell you this. Do not fight the evil person. "Imagine someone slaps you on your right cheek. Offer him your other cheek as well.
If someone takes you to court to take your shirt, give them your coat as well.
If someone forces you to go one mile, go with him two miles.
Give to the one who asks of you. Do not turn your face away from the one who wants to borrow something of yours. I love your enemies.
You have heard that it was said, "Love your neighbor" (Leviticus 19:18). "And hate your enemy."
But that is what I am saying to you. Love your enemies. Pray for those who hurt you.
Then you will be the children of your Father in heaven. For He causes His sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the just and the unjust.
If you love those who love you, what reward will you get? Even the tax collectors do that.
If you only greet your relatives, what more do you do than others? Even people who don't believe in God do that.
Be perfect, therefore, as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Providing assistance to those in need.
Be careful not to do "good deeds" in front of others. Don't do them so that others can see you. Otherwise, your Father in heaven will not reward you.
When you give to the needy, do not announce it with trumpets. Do not be like those who make a show of their piety. They announce their good deeds in the temples and on the streets, so that they may receive praise from others. But what I am about to tell you is the truth: they have already received their full reward.
When you give to the needy, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
Then your giving should be secret. Your father will reward you. He sees what you do secretly. Prayer.
When you pray, don't be like the hypocrites. They love to stand and pray in the temples and on the street corners. They want to be seen by others. What I am about to tell you is the truth. They have received their full reward.
When you pray, go into your room, close the door, and pray to your Father who is in secret. And your Father who sees what is done in secret will reward you. Your Father knows what you do in secret.
When you pray, absolutely do not speak at all like the impious do. They think that because they speak a lot, they will be answered.
Don't be like them. Your father knows what you need even before you ask him.
This is how you should pray. "Our Father who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name.
Let your kingdom come. Let your will be done on earth as in heaven.
Give us this day our daily bread.
Forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us.
Deliver us from the time of trial and from the evil one.
Forgive people when they wrong you. If you do, your Father who is in heaven will forgive you.
But if you do not forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father will not forgive your trespasses. Fast.
When you abstain from food, you do not look as gloomy as those who pretend to be pious. They make their faces look very sad. They want to show people that they are fasting. What I am about to tell you is true. They have received their full reward.
But when you fast, put oil on your head and wash your face.
Then no one will know that you are fasting, but your father who cannot be seen is the one who will reward you. Your father who sees in secret will reward you publicly. Gather treasures in heaven for yourselves.
Do not store up for yourselves treasures on earth, where moth and rust destroy, and where thieves break in and steal.
Instead, lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust destroys, and where thieves do not break in or steal.
Where your treasure is, there your heart will be also.
The eye is like a lamp for the body. If your eyes are good, your whole body will be filled with light.
But what if your eyes are bad? Then your whole body will be dark. If the light that is inside you is darkness, how great is that darkness!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. Either you will hate one and love the other, or you will be devoted to one and despise the other. You cannot serve both God and money. Don't worry.
Let me tell you, don't be worried. Don't be concerned about your life, what you will eat or drink. And don't be worried about your bodies and what you will wear. Are there not more important matters in life than eating? Are there not more important things for the body than clothes?
Consider the birds of the sky; for they neither sow nor reap, nor gather into barns. Yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Aren't you much more valuable than they?
Can you increase your life by one hour by worrying?
And why are you anxious about your clothes? Look at the field lilies, how they grow; they neither spin nor weave.
But let me tell you something. Not even Solomon in all his glory was arrayed like one of those flowers.
If this is how God adorns the wild grass, would He not adorn you even more beautifully? After all, the grass is only here today. Tomorrow it will be thrown into the fire. Your faith is very weak!
So don't worry. Don't say, "What will we eat?" or "What will we drink?" or "What will we wear?"
People who don't know God are pursued by all those things. Your Father in heaven knows you need them.
But seek first the kingdom of God. Do His will. Then all these things will be given to you as well.
So don't worry about tomorrow. Tomorrow will worry about itself. Each day has its own troubles.
Be fair when judging others.
Do not judge others, lest you be judged.
You will be judged by the same standard you judge others. You will be measured by the same measure you measure others.
You see the straw in your friend's eye and do not see the beam in your own eye.
How can you tell your friend, 'Let me take the wood shavings out of your eye'? How can you say that with a piece of wood in your own eye?
O demonstrator! First remove the beam from your own eye. Then you will be able to see clearly to remove the speck of dust from your friend's eye.
Do not give what is holy to the dogs. And do not throw your pearls before the swine, lest they trample them under their feet, and turn and tear you. Ask, seek, knock.
Ask, and it will be given to you. Seek, and you will find. Knock, and the door will be opened to you.
He who asks will receive. He who seeks will find. The door will be opened for him who knocks.
If your son asks for bread, which of you would give him a stone?
Or let's suppose he asked for a fish. Which one of you would give him a snake?
Although you are evil, you know how to give good gifts to your children. How much more, then, will your Father who is in heaven give good gifts to those who ask him!
In everything, treat others as you would like to be treated. This is what is written in the Law and the Prophets. The Great and Small Gates.
Enter the kingdom of God through the narrow gate. For wide is the gate and broad is the road that leads to destruction, and many enter through it.
But the door is narrow and the path is rough leading to life. Few people find it. The tree and its fruits.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
You will know them by their deeds. "Do people pick grapes from thorny bushes? Do they pick figs from thorny thickets?
In the same way, every good tree bears good fruit; but a bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree cannot bear bad fruit, nor can a bad tree bear good fruit.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down. It is thrown into the fire.
You can tell a tree by its fruits.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but the person who does the will of my Father who is in heaven will enter.
Many will say to me on that day, 'Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name? Did we not cast out demons in your name? Did we not do many miracles in your name?'
I will tell them clearly, "I never knew you. Away from me, you evildoers!" The wise builder and the foolish one.
Therefore, everyone who hears these words of mine and acts on them will be like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
Rain falls. Waters rise. Winds blow and beat against that house. But it does not fall. It is built on the rock.
But everyone who hears my words and does not do them is like a foolish man who built his house on sand.
Rain falls. Waters rise. Winds blow and hit that house. And it falls with a resounding crash.
Jesus finished saying all these things. And the crowds were amazed at his teaching.
He taught as one having authority. He did not speak as the teachers of the law.
Jesus heals a man with a skin disease.
Jesus went down from the mountain. And a great multitude followed him.
A man with a skin disease came and knelt before Jesus. "Lord," he said, "if you are willing, you can make me 'clean'."
Jesus reached out his hand and touched the man. And he said, "I am willing; be clean!" And immediately the leprosy left him, and he was clean. (Note: The provided translation maintains the original formatting and spacing.)
Then Jesus said to him, "Do not tell anyone, but go and show yourself to the priest and offer the gift that Moses commanded. This will be a testimony to them." The Faith of a Roman Centurion
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him. The centurion asked Jesus for help.
He said to him, "My master, my servant is bedridden at home and cannot move. He is suffering severely."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain answered and said, "Sir, I am not worthy to have you receive me in my house; but just speak the word, and my servant shall be healed."
I myself am a man under authority. And I have soldiers who obey my orders. I say to this one, "Go," and he goes. I say to that one, "Come," and he comes. I say to my servant, "Do this," and he does it.
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed. And he said to those who were following him, "Truly I tell you, I have not found such great faith, not even in Israel."
I tell you that many will come from the east and the west. And they will take their places at the feast of the kingdom of heaven. And they will eat with Abraham and Isaac and Jacob.
But the people who think they belong to the kingdom will be thrown out, into the darkness. There they will cry and grind their teeth.
Then Jesus said to the Roman officer, "Go! It will be just as you believed it would." And at that very hour, his servant was healed. Jesus healed many people.
When Jesus entered Peter's house, he saw his mother-in-law. She was lying down on the bed. And she was suffering from a fever.
Jesus touched her hand, and the fever left her. She rose and began to serve him.
When evening came, many people who were possessed by demons were brought to him. He cast out the demons by a word of command. He healed all the sick.
Following Jesus requires sacrifice.
Jesus saw the crowds around him. He gave his disciples orders to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then one of the scribes came to him and said, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered, "Foxes have dens, and birds of the air have nests; but the Son of Man has nowhere to lay his head."
Another follower said to him, "Master, first let me go and bury my father."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow Me. Let the dead bury their dead." Jesus Calms the Storm.
Jesus entered a boat. His disciples followed him.
Suddenly, a fierce storm swept over the lake. Waves began to splash over the boat. But Jesus was sleeping.
The students went and woke him up. They said, "Lord! Save us! We are sinking!"
He answered them, "How little faith you have! Why are you so afraid?" And he got up and rebuked the winds and the waves, and they subsided, and there was a calm.
The disciples were amazed. And they questioned, "What kind of man is this? Even the winds and the waves obey him!"
Jesus arrived on the other side of the lake, in the region of the Gadarenes. He encountered two men who were controlled by demons. They had come out of the tombs. The two men were so savage that no one could pass that way.
"O God's Son, what do you want from us? Have you come to punish us before the time of our trial?"
Not very far away, there was a large herd of pigs feeding.
The demons pleaded with Jesus, saying, "If you drive us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" So the evil spirits came out of the men and entered the pigs. The entire herd of pigs then rushed down the steep hillside, ran to the lake, and drowned in the water.
The men who tended the pigs fled. They went to the town and reported everything. They told the people what had happened to the men who were controlled by the demons.
Then the whole city went out to meet Jesus. When they saw him, they begged him to leave their region.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
Jesus got into a boat. He went to the other side of the lake and arrived at his own town.
Some men came to him, carrying a paralyzed man. They were laying him on a mat. Seeing their faith, Jesus said to the paralyzed man, "Cheer up, son. Your sins are forgiven."
Then one of the scribes said within himself, "This man speaks blasphemies!"
Jesus knew what they were thinking. He said to them, "Why do you think evil in your hearts?
Is it easier to say, 'Your sins are forgiven'? Or to say, 'Rise and walk'?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then he spoke to the man who could not walk. "Get up," he said. "Take your mat and go home."
The man got up and went home.
When the crowd saw this, they were filled with wonder. They praised God for giving such authority to human beings. Jesus summoned Matthew.
As Jesus was leaving that place, he saw a man named Matthew. He was sitting at the tax office. "Follow me," Jesus said to him. So Matthew got up and followed him.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at Matthew's house. Many tax collectors and "sinners" came and ate with Jesus and his disciples.
The Pharisees saw this. They asked the disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard this. And He said, "Those who are well do not need a physician; but those who are sick do need one."
Go and learn what this means: 'I desire mercy and not sacrifice' (Hosea 6:6). I did not come to call the righteous but sinners to repentance.' Jesus was asked about fasting.
One day, the disciples of John came. And they said to Jesus, "We and the Pharisees fast. But your disciples do not fast."
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom mourn while he is with them? The time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them; then they will fast."
People do not sew patches of new cloth on an old garment. For the new cloth will separate from the old, making the tear worse.
People do not put new wine into old wineskins. Otherwise, the wineskins will burst. The wine will spill out, and the wineskins will be ruined. No, everyone puts new wine into new wineskins. This way, both are preserved."
While Jesus was saying this, a leader of the synagogue came, knelt down before Jesus, and said, "My daughter has just died. But come and put your hand on her, and she will live."
Jesus rose up and followed him, and so did his disciples.
At that moment, a woman came up to Jesus from behind. She had been suffering from a disease that made her bleed, and this had been going on for 12 years. She touched the edge of his clothes.
I thought, "I just need to touch his clothes. Then I will be healed."
Jesus turned and saw her. He said to her, "Woman, do not despair," "your faith has made you well." And at that very moment, the woman was healed.
When Jesus entered the ruler's house, he saw there the flute players. And he saw the noisy crowd.
He said, "Go away. The girl is not dead. She is sleeping." But they laughed at him.
After the crowd was put outside, Jesus went in. He took the girl by her hand, and she got up.
News of what Jesus had done spread throughout the entire region. Jesus healing two blind men.
And as Jesus was leaving there, two blind men followed him, shouting and saying, "Have mercy on us, O Son of David!"
When Jesus entered the house, the blind men came to him. And he asked them, "Do you believe that I am able to do this?" And they told him, "Yes, Lord."
Then he touched their eyes. And he said, "It shall be to you as you have believed."
They could now see again. Jesus warned them severely saying, "Make sure that no one knows about this."
But they went out and spread the news. They talked about it in all that region.
While they were going out, another man was brought to Jesus. An evil spirit controlled him, and he could not speak.
When the devil was cast out, the man spoke. The crowd marveled. They said, "Such a thing has never been seen in Israel."
But the Pharisees said, "He casts out demons by the prince of demons." There are few hired laborers.
Jesus went throughout all the cities and villages. He taught in their synagogues. He preached the gospel of the kingdom. And healed all manner of sickness and disease.
When he saw the crowds, he felt a deep concern for them. They were exhausted and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "The harvest is plentiful, but the laborers are few."
Ask the Lord of the harvest to send out workers into his harvest field."
Jesus sends the twelve disciples.
Jesus called His twelve disciples to come to Him. He granted them authority to cast out demons and to heal all diseases and sicknesses.
Here are the names of the twelve apostles. The first of them is Simon Peter, and his brother Andrew. Then comes James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John.
Next come Philip and Bartholomew, also Thomas and Matthew the tax collector. There is also James the son of Alphaeus and Thaddaeus.
Their last names were Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. Judas is the one who will later betray Jesus to his enemies.
Jesus sent these twelve out with these instructions. "Don't go to where there are no Jews," he said. "And don't enter any Samaritan town."
Go instead to the house of Israel. They are like lost sheep.
And as you go, preach this message: 'The Kingdom of Heaven is near.'
Heal the sick. Raise the dead. Make those with skin diseases 'clean' again. Drive out demons. You have received freely, so give freely.
Do not carry gold, silver, or copper in your belts.
Do not take a suitcase for traveling. Do not take extra clothes, sandals, or walking sticks. The worker should be given what he needs.
When you enter a city or a village, look for someone willing to receive you. Stay at his home until your departure.
Upon entering the house, greet those who live there.
If that house welcomes you, give it the blessing of peace. And if it does not, do not bless it.
Some people may not welcome you or listen to your words. If they do not, shake the dust off your feet as you leave that house or town.
What I am about to tell you is a fact. The Day of Judgment will be easier for the people of Sodom and Gomorrah than for the people of that town.
I send you out as sheep among wolves. Therefore be wise as serpents and harmless as doves.
Beware! The men will hand you over to the local courts. They will flog you in their temples.
You will be led before governors and kings because of me. You will be witnesses for them and for the Gentiles.
But when they arrest you, don't worry about what you will say or how you will say it. At that time, you will be given the right words to say.
You will not be the speaker. The spirit of your father will speak through you.
Brothers will deliver their brothers to be killed. Fathers will deliver their sons. And sons will rise up against their fathers and kill them.
Everyone will hate you because of me. But the one who endures to the end will be saved.
When people attack you in one place, flee to another. What I am about to tell you is true. You will not finish going through the cities of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
A student does not surpass his teacher, nor a servant his master.
It suffices for the student to be like his teacher. And it suffices for the slave to be like his master. If the master of the house is a drummer, then the conduct of all the people of the house will be dancing.
Therefore, fear not your enemies. For what is secret will be revealed in public. And what is hidden will be brought to light.
What I told you in the darkness, speak of it in the light. And what you whispered in your ear, proclaim it on the housetops.
Do not fear those who kill the body and cannot kill the soul; rather, fear the one who is able to destroy both body and soul in hell.
Are not two sparrows sold for a farthing? Yet not one of them will fall to the ground apart from your Father.
He even counts the hairs on your head!
So do not be afraid. You are more valuable than many sparrows.
What about someone who says in front of others that they know me? I will also say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I know him.
But what about someone who says in front of others that they don't know me? I will say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I don't know him.
Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I have come to divide "sons and their fathers. Daughters will refuse to obey their mothers. And daughters-in-law will become hostile to their mothers-in-law.
A man's enemies will be from his own household.
Everyone who loves his father or mother more than me is not worthy of me. Everyone who loves his son or daughter more than me is not worthy of me.
He who does not bear his cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
If someone finds his life, he will lose it. But if someone loses his life for me, he will find it.
Whoever welcomes you welcomes me, and whoever welcomes me welcomes the one who sent me.
Someone who receives a prophet, as a prophet, will receive the reward of a prophet. And someone who receives a righteous person, as a righteous person, will receive the reward of a righteous person.
He says: "If someone were to give these youngsters a glass of cold water, they would be rewarded for it. What I am about to tell you is true."
Jesus and John the Baptist
Jesus finished his teaching to his twelve disciples. Then he moved to teach and preach in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard what Christ was doing, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who was to come? Or should we look for someone else?"
Jesus answered, "Go back to John. Tell him what you hear and what you see."
The blind see. The paralyzed walk. Those with skin diseases are healed. The deaf hear. The dead are resurrected. The good news is preached to the poor.
Blessed are those who do not forsake their faith because of me.
While John's disciples were leaving, Jesus began to speak to the crowd about John. He said, "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? Will you see someone swayed by the wind?"
So, what have you come out to see? A man dressed in fine clothes? No. Those who wear fine clothes are in kings' palaces.
What then did you go out into the desert to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
He is the one spoken of in the Holy Book. It is written: "I will send my messenger to you before my arrival. He will prepare the way for you." (Malachi 3:1)
What I am about to tell you is true. No one has been born who is greater than John the Baptist. Yet the least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.
For John the Baptist's days, the kingdom of heaven has been forcefully advancing. And violent people are seizing it.
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John.
If you are willing to accept that, then John is Elijah who was supposed to come.
Whoever has ears to hear, let them hear.
What are the people like in this time? They are like children, sitting in the markets, making noise to the people.
We played the nay for you. But you did not dance. We sang a funeral song for you. But you were not sad.
When John came, he did not eat or drink as you do. And people say, 'He has a demon.'
But when the Son of Man came, he ate and drank as you do. And people said, "This man is a glutton and a drunkard, and a friend of tax collectors and 'sinners.' " But wisdom is vindicated by her deeds." The towns that did not repent were subjected to a more severe punishment and destruction.
Jesus began to speak out against the cities where He had performed most of His miracles. The people had not repented of their sins. And He said,
Oh, what terrible things will happen to you, people of Chorazin! Oh, what terrible things will befall you, people of Bethsaida! If the miracles that have been performed among you had been performed in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented of their sins long ago. They would have put on black cloth and sat in ashes.
But I tell you this. The day of judgment will be more bearable for Tyre and Sidon than for you.
And as for you, Capernaum, will you be exalted to heaven? No, but you will be brought down to Hades. If the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Sodom, it would have remained to this day.
But I tell you this. The day of judgment will be easier for Sodom than for you." Comfort for every weary one.
At that time, Jesus said, "I praise you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you have hidden these things from the wise and learned, and revealed them to the little ones."
Yes, Dad. This is what I wanted.
"My father has given me everything. The father is the only one who knows the son. And the only ones who know the father are the son and those whom the son chooses to introduce him to."
Come to me, all of you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
Become my slaves and learn from me. I am gentle and free from pride. You will find rest for your souls.
My service is easy, and my burden is light.
Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
One Sabbath day, Jesus walked through the grainfields. His disciples were hungry. They began to pick the heads of grain and eat them.
The Pharisees saw this. They said to Jesus, "Look! Doing this work on the Sabbath is against the law. But your disciples are doing it anyway!"
Jesus answered, "Haven't you read what David did? He and his men were hungry.
He entered the house of God. He and his men ate the holy bread. Only the priests were allowed to eat it.
Have you not read the law? It is being told to us how the priests in the temple shall do their work on that day, every Sabbath. But they are not considered guilty.
I tell you, here is one greater than the Temple.
The inscription says, "I desire mercy, not sacrifice." (Hosea 6:6) You do not know the meaning of these words. If you did, you would not accuse the innocent.
The Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath.
After that, Jesus went to their synagogue.
There was a man who had a withered and crooked hand. The Pharisees were looking for a reason to accuse Jesus, so they asked him, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?"
He said to them, "What if one of your sheep falls into a pit on the Sabbath? Would you not reach in and pull it out?"
The man is worth more than the sheep! So the law allows us to do good on the Sabbath.
Then Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and it was restored to its normal condition, just like the other hand.
But the Pharisees went out and conspired to kill Jesus, the chosen servant of God.
Jesus was aware of the Pharisees' plans. Therefore, he left that place. Many followed him, and he healed them all from their diseases.
But he warned them not to tell anyone who he was.
This was to fulfill what had been spoken through the prophet Isaiah. It went on to say,
This is my servant whom I have chosen; this is the one I delight in. I have put my Spirit upon him; he will proclaim justice to the nations. He will not quarrel or cry out; no one will hear his voice in the streets. The meek will possess the land and will live in it undisturbed by anyone. They will build houses and live in them; they will plant vineyards and eat their fruit. No one will make them afraid; the Lord, their God, will be their shield and their helper. The Lord will establish justice throughout the land, and all the nations will put their hope in him.
He will not argue or shout. No one will hear his voice in the streets.
He will not break a bent branch. He will not extinguish a dim flame. He will make everything alright.
"The nations shall put their hope in him." (Isaiah 42:1-4) Jesus and Beelzebul
A man who was possessed by demons was brought to Jesus. The man was blind and could not speak. Jesus healed him. Then the man was able to speak and to see.
All the people were amazed and said, "Is this the Son of David?"
When the Pharisees heard this, they said, "This fellow is casting out demons by Beelzebul, the ruler of the demons."
Jesus knew what they were thinking. "Every kingdom that fights against itself will be destroyed," he said to them. "And every city or family that is divided against itself will not stand."
If Satan casts out Satan, he is at war with himself. So how then can his kingdom endure?
You say that I cast out demons by the power of Pilate. Then, by what power do your followers cast them out? Therefore, they will be your judges.
But if I cast out demons by the Spirit of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
Or consider this. How can you enter a powerful man's house and simply take what the man owns? You must first bind him. Then you can plunder his house.
Everyone who is not with me is against me. Whoever does not gather the sheep with me scatters them.
This is what I tell you: every sin and blasphemy will be forgiven people. But speaking against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
Whoever speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven; but whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven, either in this age or in the age to come.
If you plant a good tree, its fruit will be good. And if you plant a bad tree, its fruit will be bad. You can know the tree by its fruit.
O ye serpents' holes, how can ye, being evil, speak any good thing? For of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaketh.
The righteous man says good things. These things come from the goodness residing within him. And the wicked man says wicked things. These things come from the wickedness residing within him.
But let me tell you this. On the day of reckoning, people will be asked about every careless word they spoke.
You will be judged by your words or acquitted. The miraculous sign of Jonah.
Some Pharisees and teachers of the law came to Jesus. And they said to him, "Teacher, we want to see a sign from you."
He answered, saying, "Evil and unbelieving people seek a miraculous sign! But nothing will be given except the sign of Jonah."
Jonah was in the belly of a great fish for three days and three nights. Something like this will happen to the Son of Man. He will be in the tomb three days and three nights.
The men of Nineveh will stand up on the day of judgment with this generation; and the men of Nineveh will rise up in the judgment with this generation, and will make proof that this generation is guilty; the men of Nineveh repented at the preaching of Jonah; now, there is someone greater than Jonah among you.
The Queen of Sheba will rise on the day of judgment with the people present. And she will prove that they are guilty. She came from a very far place to listen to the wisdom of Solomon. And now, there is someone more important than Solomon who is here.
What happens when an evil spirit comes out of a man? It wanders around in the dry places, looking for a place to rest. But it does not find any.
Then he says, 'I will return to the house that I left.' And when he gets there, he finds the house empty. The house has been well cleaned and put in order.
"Then the evil spirit goes out and takes with it seven other spirits more evil than itself. They go and live there. And that man becomes worse off than before. That is how the evil man meets his end in these times." Jesus' mother and his brothers
While Jesus was still talking to the crowd, his mother and brothers stood outside. They wanted to talk to him.
Someone told him, "Your mother and brothers are standing outside. They want to talk to you."
Jesus answered and said to him, "Who is my mother? And who are my brothers?"
Jesus pointed to his disciples. And he said, "This is my mother! And these are my brothers!"
Everyone who does what my Father in heaven wants is my brother or sister or mother.
The Farmer's Story
On that day, Jesus left the house and sat down by the Sea of Galilee.
Large crowds gathered around him. So he entered a boat. And he sat in it. And all the people stood on the shore.
Then he began to tell them many things through stories. And he said, "A farmer went out to sow his seeds."
Scattering the seeds on the ground. Some fell on the road. The birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell in rocky places, where there wasn't much soil. The plants grew quickly, because the soil wasn't deep.
When the sun rose, it burned the plants. They dried up because they had no roots.
Other seeds fell among the thorns. The thorns grew and choked the plants.
Other seeds were sown in fertile soil. They yielded a crop a hundred, sixty or thirty times the amount that was sown.
Upon whomsoever possesses two ears, they listen.
The students came to him. And they asked him, "Why do you use stories when you talk to people?"
He answered, "I have been given the opportunity to understand the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. That has not been given to outsiders."
Those who possess this type of knowledge will be given more. In fact, they will have a great deal. And if no one has this type of knowledge, even the little that they have will be taken away.
This is why I use stories when I talk to people. I say, "They are looking, but they do not really see. They are listening, but they do not really hear or understand."
In it the words of the prophet Isaiah are fulfilled. He said: "You will hear, but not understand; you will see, but not perceive."
These people's hearts are hardened; they barely hear with their ears. They have closed their eyes. Otherwise, they might see with their eyes, hear with their ears, understand with their hearts, and repent to the Lord, who would heal them. (Isaiah 6:9,10)
Blessed are your eyes, because they see; and your ears, because they hear.
What I am about to tell you is truly real. Many prophets and righteous people have wanted to see what you are seeing. But they did not see it. They wanted to hear what you are hearing. But they did not hear it.
Listen! This is the meaning of the farmer's story.
People hear the message of the kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the evil one comes. He steals what was planted in their hearts. Those people are like the seed that was planted on the road.
Others received the seed that fell on the rocks. Those are the ones who hear the word and immediately receive it with joy.
But they have no roots. So they last only for a little while. They quickly fall away from faith when trouble or suffering comes because of the message.
Others received the seed that fell among the thorns. These are the ones who hear the word, but the anxieties of this world and the deceitfulness of wealth choke the word, and it becomes unfruitful.
But others received the seeds that fell on good ground. They are those who hear the message and understand it. They produce a crop 100, 60, or 30 times what the farmer planted." The Parable of the Weeds
Jesus told another parable to the crowd. "This is what the Kingdom of Heaven is like," he said. "A man sowed good seeds in his field.
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then left.
The wheat began to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
The owner's servants came to him. They said, "Sir, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where did these weeds come from?"
He answered, "An enemy has done this." And the servants asked him, "Do you want us to go and pull out the weeds?"
"No," the owner replied. "While you are pulling out the weeds, you might uproot the wheat along with them."
Let them grow together until harvest. Then I will tell the workers what to do. This is what I will tell them: First, gather the weeds. Tie them into bundles to be burned. Then gather the wheat. Bring it to my storage." The parables of the mustard seed and the yeast
Jesus told another parable to the crowd. He said, "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed. Someone took it and planted it in a field.
It is the smallest of all seeds. But when it grows, it becomes the largest plant in the garden. It turns into a tree. Birds come and rest on its branches.
Jesus said to them another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like leaven." And he said, "A woman took yeast and hid it in three measures of flour, until all was leavened."
Jesus spoke all these things to the crowds in parables. He did not speak to them without a parable.
The words of the prophet who spoke them were fulfilled. For he had said, "I will open my mouth in parables, I will utter hidden things from the beginning of the creation." (Psalm 78:2) Jesus Explains the Parable of the Weeds
Then Jesus left the crowd and went into the house. And his disciples came to him. They said, "Explain to us the parable of the tares in the field."
He answered, "The one who sowed the good seed is the son of man.
The field is the world. And the good seed signifies the people of the kingdom. But the tares are the people of the wicked one.
The enemy who plants them is the devil. And the harvest is the Day of Judgment. And the laborers are the angels.
Weeds are pulled up and burned in the fire. Thus will it be on the day of reckoning.
The Son of Man will send his angels. They will uproot everything that causes sin from his kingdom. They will also destroy everyone who does evil.
They will cast them into the fiery furnace. There they will weep and gnash their teeth.
Then the people of God will shine like the sun in their father's kingdom. Whoever has ears to hear, let them listen. The stories of the hidden treasure and the pearl
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. When a man found it, he hid it again, and went away, and sold all that he had, and bought that field.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant in search of fine pearls.
A pearl merchant found a pearl that was extremely valuable. So he went away and sold all that he had. And he bought that pearl. The parable of the pearl.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net. It was cast into the lake. It gathered all kinds of fish.
When it was full, the fishermen pulled it ashore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish into baskets. But the bad fish they threw away.
Thus will the Day of Judgment be. The angels will come. They will separate the people who did what is wrong from those who did what is right.
They will throw the wicked into the burning oven. There the wicked will weep and grind their teeth.
Jesus asked: "Do you understand all these things?" They answered: "Yes."
He said to them, "Every scribe who has been trained for the kingdom of heaven is like a master of a house who brings out from his treasure what is new and what is old." A prophet without honor.
Jesus finished telling these stories. Then he moved on from there.
He came to his hometown of Nazareth. There he began to teach people in their synagogues. And they were amazed. "Where did this man get this wisdom? And where did he get these miraculous powers?" they wondered.
Isn't this the son of the carpenter? Isn't his mother called Mary? Aren't his brothers called James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Aren't all his sisters with us? So where did this man get all these things from?
They never approved of him at all. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own country, and no prophet is accepted in his own home."
There were only a few miracles performed there, because the people had little faith.
John the Baptist's head is cut off.
At that time, Herod Antipas, the ruler of Galilee and Perea, heard reports about Jesus.
He told them, "This is John the Baptist. He has been raised from the dead! That is why he has the power to perform miracles."
Herod arrested John, bound him, and imprisoned him because of Herodias. She was the wife of Herod's brother Philip.
John was telling Herod, "It is against the law for you to have her."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they believed that John was a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced for Herod and his guests. She pleased Herod greatly.
He swore to her that he would give her anything she asked for.
Herodias's daughter said to her mother, "What shall I ask?" The girl said to Herod, "I want you to give me the head of John the Baptist on a platter." Herodias's daughter said to her mother, "What shall I ask?" The girl said to Herod, "I want you to give me the head of John the Baptist on a platter."
The king was very angry. But he remembered his promise and the dinner guests. So he ordered one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod put John the Baptist to death in prison.
They brought his head on a large platter and presented it to the girl. Then she carried it to her mother.
John's disciples came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus fed the five thousand.
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to be alone. So he went in a boat to a quiet place. The crowd heard about it. They followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus arrived at the shore, he saw a large crowd. He felt deep concern for them. He healed their sick.
As evening approached, the disciples came to him and said, "We have no food here, and it is late to send the crowds away so they can go and buy food in the villages."
Jesus answered, "It is not necessary for them to go. You give them something to eat."
They answered, "We have only five loaves of bread and two fish."
He said, "Bring them here."
Then Jesus instructed the people to sit on the grass. He took the five loaves and the two fish. He looked up to heaven and gave thanks. He broke the loaves into pieces. Then He gave them to the disciples. And the disciples gave them to the people.
Everyone ate and were satisfied. The disciples gathered twelve baskets full of the leftover pieces of bread.
There were about 5,000 men who ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on the water.
Immediately, Jesus instructed the disciples to get into the boat. He told them to go ahead of him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee. Then he dismissed the crowd.
After he sent them away, he went up on a mountain by himself to pray. And when evening came, he was there alone.
The ship had sailed far from the shore. The waves were hitting it hard due to the wind blowing against it.
Early in the morning, Jesus went out to the disciples. He walked on the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake and were terrified. "It's a ghost!" they cried. And they screamed in fear.
Immediately, Jesus cried out, saying, "Be of good cheer! I am he. Do not be afraid."
"Sir, are you he?" Peter asked. "If you are, tell me to come to you on the water."
Jesus said, "Come." So Peter got out of the boat. He walked on the water toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. He began to sink. He cried out, "Lord, save me!"
Immediately, Jesus stretched out His hand and caught him, saying to him, "Your faith is very weak! Why did you doubt Me?"
When they climbed into the boat, the wind died down.
And those in the boat worshiped Jesus and said, "You are truly the Son of God!"
They crossed the lake and anchored at Gennesaret.
The men who lived there recognized Jesus. So they sent a message into the surrounding countryside. People brought all their sick to Jesus.
They begged him to allow the sick to just touch the edge of his cloak. And everyone who touched him was healed.
What makes people "unclean"?
Some Pharisees and some teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. And they asked,
Why do your disciples not obey what the elders teach? Your disciples do not wash their hands before eating!
Jesus answered, "Why do you not obey the commandment of God? You prefer to follow your own teachings!"
God said, "Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16) And He also said, "He who curses his father or his mother shall be put to death." (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
But you allow people to say to their parents, 'Any help you may have received from us is a gift dedicated to God.'
Therefore, they do not need to honor their parents with their gift. You make the word of God of no effect by following your own teachings.
O prosecutors! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you. He said:
They honor me with their words, but their hearts are far from me.
Their worship means nothing to me. They teach only man-made rules.' (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowd to Him. And He said, "Hear and understand.
What enters your mouth does not make you "unclean." It is what comes out of your mouth that makes you "unclean."
Then the disciples came to him. And they asked him, "Do you know that the Pharisees were angered when they heard this?"
Jesus answered, "There are plants my Father in heaven did not plant; these will be pulled up by the roots.
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
Haven't you understood yet? Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Whatever goes into the mouth, goes into the stomach. Then it goes out of the body.
But the things that come out of the mouth come from the heart. Those are the things that make you 'unclean'.
Evil thoughts come from the heart. So also murder, adultery, and sexual immorality. Also theft, false witness, and lying.
Those are the things that make you 'unclean'. But eating without washing your hands does not make you 'unclean'. "Faith of a Canaanite Woman"
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A woman from Canaan near Tyre and Sidon came to him and cried out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on me! A demon has seized my daughter. She is suffering greatly."
Jesus did not utter a word. His disciples came to him. They begged him, saying, "Drive her away from us. She will not stop screaming behind us."
Jesus answered, "I was only sent to the lost sheep of Israel."
Then the woman fell prostrate before him. "My lord! Save me!" she said.
He answered and said, "It is not right to take the bread of the children and throw it to their dogs."
"Yes, sir," she said, but even dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' tables.
Then Jesus answered and said to her, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be to you as you want." And her daughter was healed from that hour. Jesus feeds the four thousand.
Jesus left there. He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up to a mountain and sat down.
Large crowds gathered around him. They brought with him blind and lame people. They also brought with him those who were crippled, those who could not speak, and many others. They placed them at his feet, and he healed them.
This astounded the people. Those who were speechless began to speak. The lame were healed. Those who could not walk began to walk. The blind could see. So the people praised the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to him and said, "I have compassion for these people; they have already been with me for three days and have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry, or they will faint on the way."
His disciples answered him, "There is nothing here," they said. "Where can we get enough bread to satisfy these vast crowds?"
Jesus asked: "How many loaves do you have?" They replied: "Seven, and some small fish."
Jesus ordered the crowds to sit on the ground.
Taking the seven loaves and the fish, He gave thanks to God. Then He broke them and gave them to the disciples, and the disciples distributed them to the people.
Everyone ate and were satisfied. After that, the disciples collected seven baskets of leftover fragments.
The number of men who ate was 4,000 men. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus sent the crowd away, he got into the boat. He went to the area near Magadan.
Jesus is asked for a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a miraculous sign from heaven.
He answered, saying: "In the evening you look at the sky. And you say: 'The weather will be fine. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning you say, 'Today will be stormy. The sky is red and cloudy.' You know what you see in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
Here is the translation of the provided text: "Indeed, a wicked and treacherous people are seeking a great sign. But no sign will be given to them except the sign of Jonah." Then Jesus left them and went away. The leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The pupils crossed over to the other side of the lake. They had forgotten to bring bread with them.
Jesus said to them, "Watch out for the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The pupils talked among themselves about this matter. And they said: "He must be saying this because we didn't bring any bread with us."
Jesus knew what they were saying, so he said to them, "How little faith you have! Why are you talking to each other about having no bread?"
Don't you understand yet? Don't you remember the five loaves for the five thousand? Don't you remember how many baskets you filled with broken pieces?
Have you not remembered the seven loaves that we distributed to the four thousand? Have you not remembered the number of baskets in which you gathered the leftover bread?
How can you not understand? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was not warning them about the yeast used in bread. Rather, He was warning them about the teachings of the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus went into the region of Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "Who do people say the Son of Man is?"
They answered, "Some say he is John the Baptist; others say he is Elijah; still others say he is Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
"But what about you?" he asked. "Who do you say that I am?"
Peter answered and said, "You are the Christ. You are the Son of the Living God."
Jesus answered and said, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but My Father who is in heaven.
This is what I say to you. You are Peter. And upon this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell shall not prevail against it.
I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and what you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus speaks of his coming death.
Since then, Jesus began explaining to his disciples what would happen to him. He told them that he must go to Jerusalem. There, he must suffer many things at the hands of the elders, the chief priests, and the teachers of the law. He must be killed, and then rise from the dead on the third day.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "Never, Lord!" he said. "This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to me. You do not think about the things of God, but the things of men."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples. He said, "If anyone wants to follow me, they must say no to themselves. They must take up their cross and follow me."
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he lays down his life for me, he will find it.
What good would it do a man if he gained the whole world and lost his own soul? Or what can a man give in exchange for his soul?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father with His angels, and He will reward each one according to his works.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. Some of those who are standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in His kingdom.
Jesus' appearance was transformed.
After six days, Jesus took with him Peter and James and John, the brother of James. He led them up to a high mountain. And they were all alone together.
Before them, his appearance changed. His face was shining like the sun. And his clothes became white as light.
At that moment, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will put up three tents. One for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And as Peter was speaking, behold, a cloud suddenly overshadowed them, and they looked into the cloud and behold, a voice came from the cloud saying, "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; listen to Him."
When the disciples heard this, they were terrified and fell to the ground with their faces down.
But Jesus came and touched them, saying, "Arise, and do not be afraid."
When they lifted up their eyes, they saw no one except Jesus.
They came down from the mountain. And as they were coming down, Jesus told them what to do. He said to them, "Do not tell anyone what you have seen. Wait until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Why do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "It is true. It is written that Elijah must come and make all things new."
But I tell you: Elijah has come already, and they did not recognize him, but did to him whatever they pleased. So also will the Son of Man suffer at their hands.
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus healed a boy who had a demon.
As they came up to the crowd, a man ran up to Jesus, knelt down before him.
"Sir," he said, "have mercy on my son. He is shaking so much and suffering a lot. He often falls into the fire or into the water."
I took him to your disciples. But they could not heal him."
Jesus said, "Oh people, a wicked and adulterous generation! How long shall I be with you and bear with you? Bring the boy to me here."
Jesus commanded the devil to come out of the boy, and immediately it came out. And the boy was healed in that very moment.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately. And they asked Him, "Why could we not drive out that demon?"
He answered, "Because your faith is too small. What I am about to tell you is true. If you have faith as small as a mustard seed, it is enough. You can say to this mountain, 'Be removed and cast into the sea.' And it will be done. Nothing will be impossible for you."
